1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,761
This is the complete system layout, with the latest twelve-stack magnetron inside the top-mounted sputter gun.

2
00:00:06,761 --> 00:00:16,452
We will work from the complete apparatus into the gun, open it to explain the mechanical and magnetic design, then follow gas, cooling, power and vacuum connections.

3
00:00:16,452 --> 00:00:20,563
Finally we will illustrate ignition, shutter operation and deposition.

4
00:00:20,563 --> 00:00:24,381
The chamber and custom parts use explicit nominal CAD dimensions.

5
00:00:24,381 --> 00:00:30,195
The cylinder, chillers, power equipment and selected other purchased parts are reference envelopes.

6
00:00:30,195 --> 00:00:35,775
This is a dimensioned design study, not a fabrication release or a validated operating machine.

7
00:00:37,175 --> 00:00:44,751
The rectangular chamber has two hundred millimetres of internal width and depth, with three hundred millimetres of internal height.

8
00:00:44,751 --> 00:00:48,163
That is twelve litres before subtracting internal hardware.

9
00:00:48,163 --> 00:00:50,649
The shell plates are ten millimetres thick.

10
00:00:50,649 --> 00:00:57,531
The front door is two hundred and twenty eight by three hundred and sixteen millimetres, and sixteen millimetres thick.

11
00:00:57,531 --> 00:01:04,876
It opens around the left hinge, giving access through the one hundred and eighty by two hundred and seventy millimetre opening.

12
00:01:04,876 --> 00:01:07,767
The left side stays blank for future modification.

13
00:01:07,767 --> 00:01:11,468
Two capped KF25 ports remain on the right, one high and one low.

14
00:01:11,468 --> 00:01:16,962
The gas inlet and both gauges are behind the chamber, keeping the sides free of routine wiring.

15
00:01:16,962 --> 00:01:23,150
Plate and window thicknesses here remain nominal choices requiring vacuum structural and seal verification.

16
00:01:24,550 --> 00:01:29,450
The simple substrate stage is ninety millimetres in diameter and six millimetres thick.

17
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:32,322
Its top is at height forty above the chamber floor.

18
00:01:32,322 --> 00:01:38,518
The reference substrate is fifty millimetres square and one millimetre thick, making its top height forty one.

19
00:01:38,518 --> 00:01:46,459
At the default setting the target face is at height one hundred and forty one, leaving a one hundred millimetre target to substrate distance.

20
00:01:46,459 --> 00:01:51,641
Moving the gun gives a modeled range from seventy five to one hundred and fifty millimetres.

21
00:01:51,641 --> 00:01:55,189
The shutter is one millimetre sheet with two right angle bends.

22
00:01:55,189 --> 00:01:59,976
Its blade underside is at height fifty three, twelve millimetres above the substrate.

23
00:01:59,976 --> 00:02:04,764
Its upper flat and lower blade midplanes differ by two hundred and forty millimetres.

24
00:02:04,764 --> 00:02:07,411
Turning the top actuator swings the blade away.

25
00:02:07,411 --> 00:02:13,775
These shutter clearances are geometrical, while its small rotary seal still needs a practical leak and wear test.

26
00:02:15,175 --> 00:02:17,438
Now we open the actual latest gun model.

27
00:02:17,438 --> 00:02:27,225
The protective outer shield and dark-space cap move aside so the target, target clamp, copper cooling head, magnet pack, rear closure, insulation and support become visible.

28
00:02:27,225 --> 00:02:30,676
Exploded distances are deliberately enlarged for explanation.

29
00:02:30,676 --> 00:02:34,466
They are not machining dimensions or an approved disassembly order.

30
00:02:34,466 --> 00:02:39,728
The stem carries the water hoses and a single coaxial electrical connection down to the head.

31
00:02:39,728 --> 00:02:44,876
The outer chamber envelope has not been enlarged to accommodate the new magnet arrangement.

32
00:02:44,876 --> 00:02:52,400
The complete CAD retains the fasteners, seals and small parts even where we hide them in a close-up to keep the explanation readable.

33
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:59,111
At the front is the fifty point eight millimetre diameter target, modeled two millimetres thick.

34
00:02:59,111 --> 00:03:03,150
It is held against the copper head by the target clamp and its fasteners.

35
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:07,410
The copper spreads heat away from the target into the water-cooled structure.

36
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:15,764
In the revised head the coolant channel is an annulus between radii fifteen point five and twenty point five millimetres, with seven millimetres depth.

37
00:03:15,764 --> 00:03:23,012
The revised water seals have nominal inside diameters twenty three and forty three millimetres, each with a two millimetre section.

38
00:03:23,012 --> 00:03:27,659
The two water axes move to plus and minus eighteen millimetres to suit this channel.

39
00:03:27,659 --> 00:03:32,417
The section view exposes these features, rather than making a second physical opening.

40
00:03:32,417 --> 00:03:39,775
Seal squeeze, electrical isolation, corrosion and pressure testing still need to be checked against the final materials and hardware.

41
00:03:41,175 --> 00:03:44,931
The selected arrangement uses readily available N42 disc magnets.

42
00:03:44,931 --> 00:03:52,557
Each of the twelve outer positions holds two six millimetre diameter, ten millimetre long magnets, giving a twenty millimetre stack.

43
00:03:52,557 --> 00:03:59,606
The centre contains two twenty millimetre diameter by ten millimetre magnets, also giving twenty millimetres total length.

44
00:03:59,606 --> 00:04:03,188
The outer stacks sit on a fifty eight millimetre pitch circle.

45
00:04:03,188 --> 00:04:07,868
The centre presents north toward the target and the outside stacks present south.

46
00:04:07,868 --> 00:04:15,957
Compared with the earlier outer-dominant arrangement, the larger centre and smaller outer poles move the design toward a balanced magnetron.

47
00:04:15,957 --> 00:04:20,983
Twelve positions give a more evenly distributed ring than the six-position alternative.

48
00:04:20,983 --> 00:04:24,450
The outer to centre pole-area proxy is one point zero eight.

49
00:04:24,450 --> 00:04:29,650
That is a useful selection aid, not a measured flux ratio or proof of a balanced topology.

50
00:04:31,050 --> 00:04:42,168
The copper and steel pieces were revised around this pack: a twenty one millimetre centre bore, six point four millimetre outer wells, and a seventy one point five millimetre outer diameter.

51
00:04:42,168 --> 00:04:45,504
The steel return path guides flux between opposing poles.

52
00:04:45,504 --> 00:04:53,931
A separate three dimensional nonlinear magnetostatic study evaluated this candidate using N42 remanence and an assumed AISI ten ten steel curve.

53
00:04:53,931 --> 00:05:04,698
At a quarter millimetre above the modeled nonmagnetic target, the calculated parallel field averaged ninety five point one millitesla around a racetrack near twenty millimetres radius.

54
00:05:04,698 --> 00:05:09,613
It ranged approximately eighty nine point five to ninety nine point five millitesla.

55
00:05:09,613 --> 00:05:14,002
These are field calculations under stated material and spacing assumptions.

56
00:05:14,002 --> 00:05:22,838
They do not predict ignition, deposition rate, erosion profile or plasma confinement time, and they do not by themselves certify the balanced topology.

57
00:05:22,838 --> 00:05:26,525
Copper changes the spacing; it is not a static magnetic shield.

58
00:05:27,925 --> 00:05:33,135
The live target assembly is separated from the grounded support by the insulating components.

59
00:05:33,135 --> 00:05:35,768
The single coaxial cable runs through the stem.

60
00:05:35,768 --> 00:05:41,650
Its centre conductor continues to the live terminal; its outer shield connects to the grounded structure.

61
00:05:41,650 --> 00:05:45,908
This arrangement avoids treating the coolant lines as electrical conductors.

62
00:05:45,908 --> 00:05:52,742
The model includes the shield contact, centre dielectric, added guard sleeve and terminal geometry from the latest design.

63
00:05:52,742 --> 00:05:58,960
The shield tube and front dark-space cap surround the source and protect the adjacent hardware from deposition.

64
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:04,787
They also restrict what can be seen from the front window, which is why we checked the viewing geometry.

65
00:06:04,787 --> 00:06:07,924
The five millimetre cable diameter is a study allowance.

66
00:06:07,924 --> 00:06:16,775
Cable construction, RF current path, insulation creepage, voltage rating and the sealed atmosphere-to-vacuum boundary still require engineering qualification.

67
00:06:18,175 --> 00:06:22,109
The argon cylinder and regulator are intentionally representative.

68
00:06:22,109 --> 00:06:27,534
From the regulator a six millimetre outside diameter tube runs to the mass flow controller.

69
00:06:27,534 --> 00:06:34,747
The CAD tube is modeled with a four millimetre internal bore, a chosen value rather than a tubing purchase specification.

70
00:06:34,747 --> 00:06:38,264
At the MFC inlet it enters the six millimetre push fitting.

71
00:06:38,264 --> 00:06:45,000
That fitting connects through the quarter inch NPT and female VCR adapter assembly to the assumed male VCR inlet.

72
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:51,974
The MFC outlet is also assumed male VCR and connects through the female VCR to KF25 adapter at the rear chamber port.

73
00:06:51,974 --> 00:06:56,445
The metal VCR gaskets and the independent MFC support bracket are retained.

74
00:06:56,445 --> 00:07:06,400
The regulator and all gas-contact components must ultimately suit the selected gas and operating pressure; no cylinder pressure is intended to reach the MFC unchecked.

75
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:11,969
This close-up shows the tube being pushed straight into the existing collet.

76
00:07:11,969 --> 00:07:18,169
The modeled tube outside diameter is exactly six millimetres and the fitting has a nominal six millimetre socket.

77
00:07:18,169 --> 00:07:23,161
Its mouth is at coordinate three hundred and sixty point one nine six on the rearward axis.

78
00:07:23,161 --> 00:07:30,403
In this illustration the tube end reaches three hundred and forty four point one nine six, giving sixteen millimetres of engagement.

79
00:07:30,403 --> 00:07:34,847
That sixteen millimetres is deliberately recorded as a provisional visual choice.

80
00:07:34,847 --> 00:07:41,869
We still need the actual fitting specification to set the final insertion depth and confirm tube material and gas compatibility.

81
00:07:41,869 --> 00:07:49,275
The animation shows the physical insertion direction; it does not simulate the hidden gripper teeth or an actual leak-tight connection.

82
00:07:50,675 --> 00:07:53,408
Cooling system number one serves the sputter gun.

83
00:07:53,408 --> 00:07:56,698
The blue line is the supply and the red line is the return.

84
00:07:56,698 --> 00:08:01,326
Both continue from the two existing gun water hoses at the top of the service tube.

85
00:08:01,326 --> 00:08:08,966
These external extensions keep the current four millimetre outside diameter and two point five millimetre internal bore of the gun hoses.

86
00:08:08,966 --> 00:08:13,762
The large upper service loops represent the slack needed when changing the gun height.

87
00:08:13,762 --> 00:08:21,179
Their shape is a routing study: it does not establish a permissible minimum bend radius or demonstrate adequate flow through the gun.

88
00:08:21,179 --> 00:08:23,298
The chiller cabinet is representative.

89
00:08:23,298 --> 00:08:28,819
We have not selected its heat-removal capacity, water pump, temperature setpoint or flow interlock.

90
00:08:28,819 --> 00:08:31,775
The final gun heat load must determine those choices.

91
00:08:33,175 --> 00:08:37,896
Cooling system number two is separate and connects only to the turbo water cooler.

92
00:08:37,896 --> 00:08:42,560
This keeps the turbo loop identifiable instead of mixing it with the gun circuit.

93
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:45,554
The external cabinet and routing are representative.

94
00:08:45,554 --> 00:08:56,781
The Edwards manual specifies approximately six millimetre internal diameter reinforced hose over the cooler barbs, secured with clips, or direct connections to its one eighth BSP female fittings.

95
00:08:56,781 --> 00:08:59,948
The final tube and adapters must follow that interface.

96
00:08:59,948 --> 00:09:02,769
Either cooler barb can serve as supply or return.

97
00:09:02,769 --> 00:09:09,909
The cooling controller must coordinate water flow with pump operation, including avoiding condensation when the pump is off.

98
00:09:09,909 --> 00:09:14,400
No cooling flow rate, pressure or duty is being claimed by this routing model.

99
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:20,099
The representative power supply feeds the gun through the external cable.

100
00:09:20,099 --> 00:09:25,751
We show an RF configuration, including a separate matching network between generator and source.

101
00:09:25,751 --> 00:09:30,698
RF sputtering uses the matching network to couple power into a changing plasma load.

102
00:09:30,698 --> 00:09:38,235
If the final system uses DC, the DC supply connects through the appropriate cable arrangement and bypasses this RF matching box.

103
00:09:38,235 --> 00:09:42,357
The two options are alternatives, not two supplies operating together.

104
00:09:42,357 --> 00:09:48,363
Conventional DC is suited to electrically conductive targets, while RF can support insulating targets.

105
00:09:48,363 --> 00:09:52,367
The green bonding lead is a visual reminder of protective grounding.

106
00:09:52,367 --> 00:09:55,076
It is not a completed grounding specification.

107
00:09:55,076 --> 00:10:03,025
Cooling flow, chamber pressure, enclosure access and supply enable need coordinated interlocks before this becomes an operating system.

108
00:10:04,425 --> 00:10:08,845
The EXT70 reference mounts below the chamber through the ISO63 connection.

109
00:10:08,845 --> 00:10:13,861
Four representative double-claw clamps hold the mating flanges around the face seal.

110
00:10:13,861 --> 00:10:20,073
The turbo body is reconstructed from the manufacturer drawing rather than an exact supplier solid model.

111
00:10:20,073 --> 00:10:26,642
The reference version has a KF16 backing outlet, so the model includes a KF16 to KF25 adapter before the hose.

112
00:10:26,642 --> 00:10:31,241
If the actual purchased variant already has a KF25 outlet, omit that adapter.

113
00:10:31,241 --> 00:10:38,528
The chamber stand carries the chamber independently; the turbo is not being used as the structural support for the vessel.

114
00:10:38,528 --> 00:10:46,650
The inlet baffle above it helps interrupt a direct deposition path, although its conductance and effectiveness still require assessment.

115
00:10:48,050 --> 00:10:51,187
We retained the simpler plumbing option you selected.

116
00:10:51,187 --> 00:10:57,402
There is one route from chamber through turbo, then through the backing hose to a single mechanical pump.

117
00:10:57,402 --> 00:11:00,006
There is no separate roughing bypass branch.

118
00:11:00,006 --> 00:11:07,109
The backing pump model is supplier CAD for an Edwards RV3, used as a reference envelope rather than a purchase decision.

119
00:11:07,109 --> 00:11:10,246
The KF25 hose connects to its inlet, not its exhaust.

120
00:11:10,246 --> 00:11:14,448
Its modeled centreline is about six hundred and forty millimetres long.

121
00:11:14,448 --> 00:11:19,065
Flow markers illustrate the evacuation direction, not a pump-down calculation.

122
00:11:19,065 --> 00:11:23,918
Pump startup and venting must follow the controller and manufacturer instructions.

123
00:11:23,918 --> 00:11:30,133
This arrangement does not imply the turbo can stay at operating speed while the chamber is freely opened.

124
00:11:30,133 --> 00:11:36,525
Backing pump exhaust treatment, oil mist handling and the turbo controller are further equipment to specify.

125
00:11:37,925 --> 00:11:43,224
The Pirani and Penning gauge connections occupy the two lower rear KF25 ports, through elbows.

126
00:11:43,224 --> 00:11:49,593
These break the direct line of sight from the target toward the sensor bodies and reduce direct coating exposure.

127
00:11:49,593 --> 00:11:52,355
Their housings here are generic reference shapes.

128
00:11:52,355 --> 00:12:01,994
We have not yet selected the gauge models or controllers, so final mounting orientation, sensitivity and allowable stray magnetic field must be checked from their manuals.

129
00:12:01,994 --> 00:12:04,644
The upper rear port receives the MFC gas inlet.

130
00:12:04,644 --> 00:12:10,788
Keeping these services at the rear gives the system a cleaner working side and preserves the blank left wall.

131
00:12:10,788 --> 00:12:19,525
Gauge readings, MFC control and pressure interlocks belong in the eventual control system, even though the current CAD is focused on the mechanical layout.

132
00:12:20,925 --> 00:12:28,132
The original window centre was at height one hundred and seventy five, above the default target face at one hundred and forty one.

133
00:12:28,132 --> 00:12:32,733
The shield blocked all sampled rays toward a ring five millimetres below that face.

134
00:12:32,733 --> 00:12:38,387
We therefore lowered the same seventy millimetre clear aperture to centre height one hundred and five.

135
00:12:38,387 --> 00:12:49,364
At an observer position four hundred millimetres in front and height twenty above the floor datum, forty seven of seventy two sampled rays now reach that geometric ring without intersecting the gun.

136
00:12:49,364 --> 00:12:52,302
A still lower viewing angle exposes more of the ring.

137
00:12:52,302 --> 00:12:57,513
This is a direct triangle-intersection sightline check, not a prediction of plasma brightness.

138
00:12:57,513 --> 00:13:02,779
The screen shows the opaque door and shield, with illustrative glow behind the actual aperture.

139
00:13:02,779 --> 00:13:05,828
It establishes useful access at the default gun height.

140
00:13:05,828 --> 00:13:11,150
It does not guarantee an equally clear view at every gun position or after the window is coated.

141
00:13:12,550 --> 00:13:17,650
Here the glow and particles are an illustration of the process, not a simulated discharge.

142
00:13:17,650 --> 00:13:23,090
After evacuation and controlled argon admission, electrical power creates and sustains a plasma.

143
00:13:23,090 --> 00:13:28,814
Argon ions accelerate through the sheath toward the target and knock target atoms out of its surface.

144
00:13:28,814 --> 00:13:37,768
The magnetic field curves electron motion and helps keep electrons near the target, increasing ionization locally and producing the familiar racetrack region.

145
00:13:37,768 --> 00:13:42,641
The magnetic field does not simply pull all of the sputtered atoms onto the substrate.

146
00:13:42,641 --> 00:13:50,178
Many ejected target atoms are neutral and travel through the gas toward the substrate and chamber surfaces, with possible collisions.

147
00:13:50,178 --> 00:13:52,955
The coloured moving dots are explanatory symbols.

148
00:13:52,955 --> 00:14:00,775
Their sizes, speeds, numbers and paths are exaggerated and do not represent pressure, deposition rate or a calculated energy distribution.

149
00:14:02,175 --> 00:14:07,754
With the shutter closed, the source can be conditioned before intentionally exposing the substrate.

150
00:14:07,754 --> 00:14:11,247
The blade intercepts the direct deposition path to the sample.

151
00:14:11,247 --> 00:14:16,939
It does not stop the plasma, seal off the substrate or prevent every scattered atom from reaching it.

152
00:14:16,939 --> 00:14:21,109
The illustrated atom paths terminate at the blade in this simplified view.

153
00:14:21,109 --> 00:14:24,377
Opening the shutter then exposes the sample to the source.

154
00:14:24,377 --> 00:14:36,267
This is a conceptual sequence, not an operating recipe: actual base pressure, argon flow, working pressure, power, warm-up time and conditioning duration need experimental development within the hardware limits.

155
00:14:36,267 --> 00:14:40,775
Cooling and pressure permissives must be established before enabling the source.

156
00:14:42,175 --> 00:14:47,519
The shutter rotates eighty degrees away and the illustrated target atoms now reach the substrate.

157
00:14:47,519 --> 00:14:52,203
The sample is one hundred millimetres below the target in this default configuration.

158
00:14:52,203 --> 00:14:55,343
A coloured layer appears on it to explain film formation.

159
00:14:55,343 --> 00:15:00,357
Its displayed thickness is exaggerated for visibility and must not be read as a prediction.

160
00:15:00,357 --> 00:15:09,558
Changing the source distance changes the geometry, but the present model does not calculate thickness uniformity, deposition rate, gas scattering or substrate heating.

161
00:15:09,558 --> 00:15:14,903
Those depend on the operating conditions and require measurement or an appropriate process model.

162
00:15:14,903 --> 00:15:21,900
This demonstration explains the sequence while preserving the actual chamber, stage, source and shutter geometry underneath it.

163
00:15:23,300 --> 00:15:40,605
This brings the full mechanical system and the operating idea into one walkthrough: the twelve-stack source, adjustable compression mount, loading door and window, substrate stage and shutter, ISO63 turbo, single backing pump, gauges, MFC, argon supply, two cooling systems and power equipment.

164
00:15:40,605 --> 00:15:46,550
The CAD package records the actual modeled dimensions and the reference status of purchased hardware.

165
00:15:46,550 --> 00:16:01,854
The main remaining work is to confirm purchased interfaces and material ratings, resolve the source sealing details, check the vessel and window for vacuum loading, and specify the electrical controls, cooling interlocks, turbo controller and exhaust handling.

166
00:16:01,854 --> 00:16:06,681
The imported backing-pump reference also retains a known supplier geometry defect.

167
00:16:06,681 --> 00:16:10,271
None of these are hidden by the illustrative plasma sequence.

168
00:16:10,271 --> 00:16:16,275
The result is a complete system layout and explanation, with its engineering limits stated explicitly.
